---
import ArticleLayout from "@layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
---

<ArticleLayout
  title="Pilot"
  description="Pilot is a self-hosted planning workspace where the documentation is the source of work, and durable Pi agents implement against it. Open source, single owner, built with Svelte and Node."
  subtitle="Documentation is the source of work."
  eyebrow="2026 · Self-hosted / Open source"
  tags="Svelte 5 · Node · Pi Durable · SQLite"
>
  <figure slot="art" class="pilot-art">
    <img
      src="/assets/pilot/wiki.jpg"
      alt="Pilot rendering docs/ARCHITECTURE.md: a file list down the left and the document, with its surfaces table and numbered invariants, on the right"
      width="1672"
      height="1045"
      fetchpriority="high"
    />
  </figure>

  <p class="lead">
    Pilot is a workspace for the work rather than the code. Write the intent
    first — behaviour, decisions, open questions, diagrams — then let a durable
    agent implement against that page. The plan, the conversation and the result
    stay in one place, reachable from a phone, with the repository still owning
    the truth.
  </p>
  <a
    class="text-link"
    href="https://github.com/goodjobdev/pilot"
    target="_blank"
    rel="noopener noreferrer"
  >
    View the repository on GitHub ↗
  </a>

  <section>
    <h2>Write the intent first</h2>
    <p>
      The wiki browses, edits, searches and renders any Markdown inside a
      registered project: diagrams, images, static HTML mockups, specification
      links and an inbox of the open questions still waiting for an answer.
      Writes are revision-checked, so a second writer gets a conflict that keeps
      their draft instead of a file that quietly loses the first edit.
    </p>
    <p>
      Markdown stays the source. The index, the canvas, the alert subscriptions
      and the named-session registry are all derived or working state, so
      deleting them costs convenience and never documentation. Every read and
      write resolves through one containment helper that refuses absolute paths,
      parent directory hops and symlinks out of the project.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Agents that work from the page</h2>
    <p>
      Each project can hold one durable Pi Durable session, committed to SQLite
      as it runs: a live transcript, steering, stopping, and questions the agent
      asks that you answer once. A run that dies mid-tool resumes from its last
      checkpoint, and a tool that may have partially run is reported as
      interrupted rather than repeated.
    </p>
    <p>
      Work is admitted durably before it is shown. An input is committed to
      storage first and the interface reads committed state, so closing the
      browser cannot lose a run. Web Push carries the questions, finished runs
      and failures to the phone, with a link that opens the exact project.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The board reads the documentation</h2>
    <p>
      Work items live in <code>docs/dashboard/board.md</code> inside the
      project, so the dependency canvas, the milestone timeline and the task
      list are views over a file in git. A task can point at the specification
      it implements; a task without a link is flagged rather than blocked.
    </p>
    <figure class="pilot-board">
      <img
        src="/assets/pilot/tasks.jpg"
        alt="The Pilot task list: twelve work items with area, state, specification link, milestone and the tasks blocking each one"
        width="1672"
        height="1045"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>
        The task list for Pilot itself, planned in Pilot. Specification links
        and blocking relationships come from the board file in the repository.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What it is not</h2>
    <p>
      Pilot is single owner by design: one person, one credential, no roles, no
      sharing and no rate limiting. The agent harness is code execution — agent
      tools are bounded to a project workspace, and a project you register is a
      directory the agent may write to, so it belongs in a container rather than
      on a workstation. The backup script covers documentation and deliberately
      excludes sessions, alert keys and credentials.
    </p>
    <p>
      An unclean kill leaves a lock, and the application refuses to start until
      an operator clears it — fail-closed, but a crash-loop until someone
      intervenes. The default provider is a scripted local stand-in, and the
      agent tools are question and note operations rather than a complete coding
      toolchain.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Running it</h2>
    <p>
      Pilot needs Node 24 or later, because it uses the unflagged
      <code>node:sqlite</code>. Local mode binds loopback and embeds a session
      token in the page it serves, so it must never be exposed through a tunnel.
    </p>
    <pre><code>pnpm install
pnpm check &amp;&amp; pnpm test &amp;&amp; pnpm build
DASHBOARD_MODE=local DASHBOARD_DATA=/tmp/pilot-data PORT=4317 pnpm start</code></pre>
    <a
      class="text-link"
      href="https://github.com/goodjobdev/pilot#quick-start"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >
      Quick start, deployment and the security model on GitHub ↗
    </a>
  </section>

  <aside class="project-status">
    Working tool · MIT licensed, self-hosted, and deployed for one owner.
    Screenshots are the real interface, with the planning mirror for Pilot’s own
    documentation as the project.
  </aside>
  <a class="text-link" href="/journal/pi-gatekeeper/">
    Related: pi-gatekeeper, the verification gate for a coding agent ↗
  </a>
</ArticleLayout>

<style>
  .pilot-art,
  .pilot-board {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .pilot-art img,
  .pilot-board img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 4px;
  }
  .pilot-board {
    margin-top: 32px;
  }
  figcaption {
    margin-top: 12px;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 10px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
</style>
